{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":533688,"duration_api_ms":630663,"num_turns":41,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"0263143d-d53c-4340-8cbe-e47689c4f0aa","total_cost_usd":2.3853829500000003,"usage":{"input_tokens":1711,"cache_creation_input_tokens":95824,"cache_read_input_tokens":2029247,"output_tokens":17305,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":95824,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":1711,"outputTokens":17305,"cacheReadInputTokens":2029247,"cacheCreationInputTokens":95824,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":2.0547035,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000},"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":167,"outputTokens":22926,"cacheReadInputTokens":595512,"cacheCreationInputTokens":125065,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.33067945,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"amendments":[{"body":"In `nitrogen_budget_components()` (budget.rs:170-262) and `carbon_budget_components()` (budget.rs:272-347), the shrimp adults and juveniles are tracked as separate components, but each calls `shrimp_nitrogen_mg(adults, 0, ratio)` and `shrimp_nitrogen_mg(0, juveniles, ratio)`. Internally, `shrimp_biomass_g()` is `adults * ADULT_BIOMASS + juveniles * JUVENILE_BIOMASS`, which is linear, so splitting works correctly. However, the component labels are `animal.adults_count` and `animal.juveniles_count` which are integer count fields, not mass fields. The structural coverage test `is_shared_budget_path` hard-codes these labels in a `matches!()` arm (budget_tracking.rs:248-249). If a new animal subpopulation field is added (e.g. `animal.larvae_count`), the structural test would silently miss it since there is no naming-convention-based discovery rule for count-based mass pools. Consider adding a convention suffix check (e.g. `_count` under `animal.`) or a doc comment in the AnimalState struct flagging which count fields carry implicit mass.","rationale":"The structural coverage test catches new dissolved-pool fields via `_mg_n_total`/`_mg_c_total` suffixes and new biomass fields via `biomass_g`, but count-based mass pools (where mass is derived from count × fixed biomass) are only caught by exact match in `is_shared_budget_path`. This is a maintenance hazard: a future developer adding a new counted population pool would not be warned by the test suite that the pool is missing from the budget."},{"body":"In `apply_water_change_with_budget()` (water_change.rs:109-118), the nitrogen and carbon export calculations include the suspended algae contribution (`algae_nitrogen_mg(state.algae.suspended_biomass_g)` and `algae_carbon_mg(state.algae.suspended_biomass_g, n_to_c_ratio)`) because suspended algae are removed proportionally by water changes. However, the `dissolved_feed_residue_g_total` that is also removed by the water change (line 60: `*= retention`) is NOT included in the budget export delta. While `dissolved_feed_residue_g_total` is intentionally excluded from the budget totals (because it mirrors DOC/DON already tracked), this means the residue counter will drift from reality over time: DOC/DON are reduced by the water change and the budget correctly tracks that loss, but the residue shadow counter is also reduced without corresponding accounting. This has zero impact on mass conservation but makes the residue counter unreliable for diagnostics. Add a brief comment at water_change.rs:60 noting that this reduction is a bookkeeping adjustment only (not tracked in budget) to prevent future developers from mistakenly adding it to the budget delta.","rationale":"The `dissolved_feed_residue_g_total` exclusion from the budget is correct and well-documented in the test (budget_tracking.rs:356-359), but water_change.rs modifies it without any comment explaining the accounting rationale. A developer debugging feed-residue tracking could waste time investigating this apparent gap."},{"body":"The `test_total_n_helper_sums_all_pools` and `test_total_c_helper_sums_all_pools` tests (budget_tracking.rs:262-323) compute expected values by summing detritus pools together: `manual_organic_nitrogen_mg(state.detritus.particulate_organics_g_total + state.detritus.fine_detritus_g_total, ratio)`. This works because the conversion functions are linear, but the test would not catch a bug where `nitrogen_budget_components` assigns different N:C ratios or conversion formulas to particulate vs. fine detritus. Consider computing the two detritus contributions separately in the test to independently verify that each component's conversion is correct.","rationale":"The structural coverage test verifies that all field labels are present, but the manual sum test is the one place that verifies the actual numeric conversion formulas. By lumping detritus pools together, it relies on linearity rather than testing per-component correctness, which slightly weakens the test's ability to catch formula bugs in individual components."}],"summary":"Thorough review of the budget ledger implementation across budget.rs, engine.rs, invariants.rs, state.rs, and all system files (nitrogen_cycle, chemistry, dissolved_oxygen, shrimp, plant_growth, algae_growth, microfauna, water_change). All 18 budget_tracking tests and the full test suite pass. Clippy is clean. The core architecture is sound: BudgetTotals/BudgetSnapshot/BudgetDelta correctly implement per-tick mass tracking; total_nitrogen() and total_carbon() are defined canonically in one place and delegate to nitrogen_budget_components/carbon_budget_components which cover all N/C-bearing pools including substrate nutrient stores; the budget guard correctly separates closed-system conservation checking from open-system fluxes (feed, water change, siphon, add/remove shrimp) and atmospheric carbon exchange; the stoichiometric conversions between plant/algae/detritus/live-biomass representations are algebraically consistent (verified the N→mass→N round-trip identity). The zero-cost-when-disabled property is satisfied via Option<BudgetLedger> gating. No critical bugs found. Three minor amendments proposed around structural test coverage for future maintenance robustness."},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"724ed32b-96ca-4e19-bd45-55670ad33fe2"}
